{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's mechanism assumes that a 44-comparator 13-input sorting network must embed a 39-comparator 12-input sorting network as a contiguous prefix. However, sorting networks do not require their prefixes to optimally sort any subset of inputs; a 13-input network of 44 comparators might never sort any 12-input subset until the final comparators. Therefore, demonstrating that no optimal 12-input prefix can be extended with 5 comparators only proves this specific construction method fails, not that 44-comparator networks do not exist or that the search 'stalls at 45'. The target's own countercondition acknowledges a 44-comparator network might not embed an optimal 12-input prefix, invalidating the premise that testing such prefixes meaningfully constrains the search space.","target":"3951301d363e5ae23af4ef7e51abfb5a8df935eea4cb4331305ba81a78e60291"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target describes a sandboxed Python simulation to identify and iteratively prune redundant comparators from Batcher's network, but provides it only in prose with an empty simulation_refs channel. The problem context explicitly states: 'a simulation described in prose with an empty simulation_refs channel is an unverified claim and will be criticized as such.' Without the executable simulation to verify that Batcher's 48-comparator network can actually be reduced to 45 comparators by this greedy method, the claim that a 45-or-fewer network can be constructed this way remains unsupported.","target":"f96f334c7f7adc45fc062ec6b3afd4df1e26f818923cef1c80aad4586283e55d"}]}